ge on medical grounds, small pot lump sum if pot worth £10,000 or less, flexi-access drawdown available.","discrepancies":["Report asserts retirement age 75 with projections to age 75, but metadata notes ambiguity: report states 'expect to retire within next two years' yet cashflow models continued HGV driving income through ages 74/75, suggesting possible working to age 71-72 rather than clear retirement at 75.","Report frames recommendation as meeting 'retirement objectives in isolation' and aligned with 'stated attitude to investment risk' but does not explicitly name which specific objective each recommendation element serves.","Report projects investment growth needed to age 75 (critical yield analysis) but client currently aged 69; the 6-year investment horizon to age 75 is stated, yet access terms show Aviva drawdown not possible after age 75, creating a hard deadline that aligns with but is not explicitly reconciled with the stated retirement age.","Report recommends transfer of multiple schemes (Aviva £3,744, Zurich £103,439, Standard Life £3,002, totalling £110,185) into single Aviva plan but does not explicitly address how consolidation serves stated objectives or improves access relative to current arrangements."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The check requires assessment of whether recommendations are appropriate given stated goals, access needs, term and foreseeable changes. The report asserts a retirement target of age 75 with a £1,700 net monthly income need. Evidence supports this income need (P1, P8). The investment term (age 69 to 75, six years) is consistent between report and evidence. However, several appropriateness concerns emerge: (1) Retirement timing ambiguity: the extraction metadata notes the report states 'expect to retire within next two years' but also models continued HGV income through age 74/75. This creates uncertainty about whether the stated retirement age 75 is firm or aspirational, affecting whether a six-year growth projection to age 75 is the correct term. (2) Access mechanism tension: Aviva terms (P4, P2) explicitly state 'Moving to income drawdown isn't possible after age 75.' If the client reaches age 75 and has not crystallised drawdown, access becomes impossible. The recommendation projects to age 75 but does not discuss this hard deadline or why crystallisation before age 75 is assured. (3) Boilerplate linkage: the report states recommendations ensure 'a diversified investment portfolio based on your retirement objectives in isolation' and are 'in line with your stated attitude to investment risk' but does not name the specific objective(s) each component serves. This is boilerplate alignment language, not specific objective linkage. (4) Consolidation appropriateness: the report recommends consolidating three existing schemes into one Aviva plan but does not explain how this consolidation serves the stated £1,700/month income objective or improves access relative to leaving schemes in place. The existing Aviva scheme (£3,744) already sits on the Aviva platform; adding Zurich and Standard Life transfers does not change access mechanics but adds execution risk. (5) Foreseeable life changes: the report models continued HGV employment income through the projection period, which is appropriate, but does not explicitly address the foreseeable change from employment to non-employment income at retirement or how the selected funds' volatility profile (risk rating 5, balanced) suits the transition from capital growth to capital drawdown required at the hard deadline of age 75. On the guard for access mechanisms: a pension in the name of a 69-year-old carries no inherent access restriction—the restriction arises from the specific terms of the Aviva product (no drawdown after age 75), which is not a general feature of pensions held at age 69. This is a real constraint specific to this recommendation, not a spurious mismatch. The boilerplate guard applies: the recommendation is linked to objectives only by general statements that it meets retirement needs and risk objectives, not by naming specific objectives and explaining how each element serves them.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":"Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement."},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700"},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The cashflow runs until you reach age 100 and assumes all surplus income throughout your lifetime is spent, rather than saved."},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"From the normal minimum pension age, you can make withdrawals through income drawdown while your pension fund remains invested. The normal minimum pension age is currently age 55."},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Terms and conditions (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Moving to drawdown is available for this plan from age 55 (57 from 6 April 2028). We allow ad hoc payments to be taken, but don't offer a regular withdrawal facility. Moving to income drawdown isn't possible after age 75."},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan."},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"This will ensure you have a diversified investment portfolio based on your retirement objectives in isolation as you requested and one which is in line with your stated attitude to investment risk."}],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

